]\n    (model:request (data:record :id uid :messages msgs)))))\n(-> (seq:last conv:user-ids) (cell:input (data:get :model-output memory-cell)))\n(-> (seq:last conv:user-times) (cell:input (data:get :model-output memory-cell)))\n(-> conv:user-latest (cell:input (data:get :model-output memory-cell)))\n(-> (seq:last conv:user-ids) (cell:input (data:get :model-output memory-cell)))", :thinking "Let me analyze this complex task carefully. I need to construct a live conversation network in Lain that:\n\n1. Connects user-ingress to model requests\n2. Builds history with temporal reducers (behavior:scan, behavior:map, behavior:event-filter)\n3. Preserves chronological user messages and acknowledged assistant replies\n4. Defines a reusable reply function that delivers to user and adds acknowledged reply to history\n5. Subsequent requests contain actual prior user messages and delivered assistant replies as role-tagged messages\n6. Each model response must be executable Lain calling the reply function\n7. Include a system message with the reply-function calling convention\n8. Use different name for conversation accumulator (not \"history\")\n9. Don't send request until user event arrives\n10. Avoid cycles requiring future reply before first request\n11. model:request is a function, must be invoked not used as connection target\n12. Use cell:input on memory references for feedback\n\nKey constraints from the language introduction:\n- Lain is a connected dataflow language, not Clojure\n- Raw parenthesized forms only, no markdown fences\n- (f x) invokes callable; (-> x f) writes to cell\n- seq:empty, seq:append, seq:concat for vectors\n- event:point captures temporal occurrences\n- behavior:scan, map, event-filter produce behavior objects\n- behavior:latest-value projects to ordinary cell\n- model:request [record] submits to model\n- message-to-user [record] acknowledges delivery\n- user-ingress is a message RECORD, not behavior\n- Need event:point to capture user-ingress occurrences\n- (define name expression) binds a cell\n- (network [x y] expression) defines callable\n- (let [x expr y expr] body) binds locals\n- (-> value target-cell) connects cells\n- data:record for immutable records\n- data:get reads from records\n- Memory record fields contain references, write through cell:input\n\nLet me think about the architecture:\n\n1. **User ingress → temporal source**: user-ingress is a record. I need to capture its occurrences with event:point. But wait - user-ingress is a cell that publishes records. I need to connect it to a behavior:source via event:point. But event:point takes [id numeric-time value]. The user-ingress publishes records with :id, :time, :role, :content.\n\nActually, re-reading: \"user-ingress publishes a record with :id, :time (odd integer), :role user, :content.\" So user-ingress is a cell that outputs records. I need to capture these as temporal occurrences.\n\nBut event:point takes [id numeric-time value] - it's a static occurrence. For dynamic occurrences from a cell, I need to connect the cell's output to a behavior:source.\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"Temporal sources accept occurrences from event:point, never bare message records or other behavior objects.\"\n\nHmm, but user-ingress is a cell. How do I connect it to a behavior:source? Let me think...\n\nActually, looking at the examples: `(-> (event:point \"source-event\" 1 7) doc:source)` - event:point creates a temporal occurrence and connects it to a source.\n\nFor user-ingress, which is a cell that publishes records over time, I think I need to connect it to a behavior:source. But the language says \"Temporal sources accept occurrences from event:point, never bare message records.\"\n\nWait, let me re-read more carefully. The user-ingress cell publishes records. Each publication is an occurrence. I think the way to handle this is:\n\nActually, looking at the construction-review-cell: \"A bare acknowledged record must be converted to a temporal occurrence before 